{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":27777,"name":"Jordan Mack","username":"jm9k","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/talk.nervos.org/jm9k/{size}/3197_2.png","created_at":"2026-11-24T00:42:04.805Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eHow we do agentic engineering at Sōnami has evolved over the last few months. We wanted to share the “Crossfire Loop”, a method proven to yield the most favorable results for us.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003chr\u003e\n\u003ch1\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27777-crossfire-loop-you-are-the-bottleneck-1\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27777-crossfire-loop-you-are-the-bottleneck-1\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eCrossfire Loop: You Are the Bottleneck\u003c/h1\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSoftware engineering used to operate at a slower pace. Difficult architectural decisions were followed by an extended period of implementation and testing. AI is taking over the implementation and testing. Now the job of a software engineer is only the difficult parts, condensed into a non-stop, mentally taxing stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou are the bottleneck.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHuman intellect is still required for any serious engineering task, and it is the scarcest resource. Scaling means minimizing human involvement to only what is absolutely essential.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Crossfire Loop is the agentic engineering solution we use at Sōnami. A panel of different AI models reviews the work. The models attack each other’s findings and work together to push the work as far as it can go before a human ever looks at it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27777-different-models-different-blind-spots-2\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27777-different-models-different-blind-spots-2\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eDifferent Models, Different Blind Spots\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEvery model has strengths and weaknesses. One model is good at finding race conditions, another notices a missing test, and a third reads the spec more closely. When one model reviews its own work, it misses the same things it missed while writing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"lightbox-wrapper\"\u003e\u003ca class=\"lightbox\" href=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/original/2X/7/7f4aefeb3e3debf159220a04272d6132d12a01c0.png\" data-download-href=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/7f4aefeb3e3debf159220a04272d6132d12a01c0\" title=\"Six bugs in one change. Each of three models catches three bugs, but different ones. Together they catch five of the six.\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/7/7f4aefeb3e3debf159220a04272d6132d12a01c0_2_690x355.png\" alt=\"Six bugs in one change. Each of three models catches three bugs, but different ones. Together they catch five of the six.\" data-base62-sha1=\"ia5bPTZKVdeUywIe5ltLVFtb4Yg\" width=\"690\" height=\"355\" srcset=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/7/7f4aefeb3e3debf159220a04272d6132d12a01c0_2_690x355.png, https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/7/7f4aefeb3e3debf159220a04272d6132d12a01c0_2_1035x532.png 1.5x, https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/original/2X/7/7f4aefeb3e3debf159220a04272d6132d12a01c0.png 2x\" data-dominant-color=\"F0F4F6\"\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"meta\"\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-far-image svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#far-image\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003cspan class=\"filename\"\u003eSix bugs in one change. Each of three models catches three bugs, but different ones. Together they catch five of the six.\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"informations\"\u003e1040×536 25.4 KB\u003c/span\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-discourse-expand svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#discourse-expand\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTheir blind spots differ, so a panel from different model families catches more. That has a cost. A panel uses several times the tokens of a single reviewer.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27777-the-loop-3\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27777-the-loop-3\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eThe Loop\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"lightbox-wrapper\"\u003e\u003ca class=\"lightbox\" href=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/original/2X/8/8cbd888469e8c9ee8ef85a8c4388ed4f12fd2e83.png\" data-download-href=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/8cbd888469e8c9ee8ef85a8c4388ed4f12fd2e83\" title=\"One model builds. Models from three companies review the change on their own. They look for bugs, scope creep, and over-engineering. Then the same models attack each other's findings, and two of five findings fall. One judge model asks of each remaining issue: is it real, is it in scope, and is the fix over-engineered? Then it rules fix, reject, or defer. Fixes go back to the implementer and the loop repeats. When nothing serious is left, the work goes to human review.\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/8/8cbd888469e8c9ee8ef85a8c4388ed4f12fd2e83_2_397x500.png\" alt=\"One model builds. Models from three companies review the change on their own. They look for bugs, scope creep, and over-engineering. Then the same models attack each other's findings, and two of five findings fall. One judge model asks of each remaining issue: is it real, is it in scope, and is the fix over-engineered? Then it rules fix, reject, or defer. Fixes go back to the implementer and the loop repeats. When nothing serious is left, the work goes to human review.\" data-base62-sha1=\"k52UYIGQ8DEYXEdOPnvFTyuFU0b\" width=\"397\" height=\"500\" srcset=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/8/8cbd888469e8c9ee8ef85a8c4388ed4f12fd2e83_2_397x500.png, https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/8/8cbd888469e8c9ee8ef85a8c4388ed4f12fd2e83_2_595x750.png 1.5x, https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/8/8cbd888469e8c9ee8ef85a8c4388ed4f12fd2e83_2_794x1000.png 2x\" data-dominant-color=\"F3F5F7\"\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"meta\"\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-far-image svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#far-image\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003cspan class=\"filename\"\u003eOne model builds. Models from three companies review the change on their own. They look for bugs, scope creep, and over-engineering. Then the same models attack each other's findings, and two of five findings fall. One judge model asks of each remaining issue: is it real, is it in scope, and is the fix over-engineered? Then it rules fix, reject, or defer. Fixes go back to the implementer and the loop repeats. When nothing serious is left, the work goes to human review.\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"informations\"\u003e1040×1308 93.1 KB\u003c/span\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-discourse-expand svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#discourse-expand\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eBuild.\u003c/strong\u003e The implementer writes the change. Tests must pass before any review.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReview.\u003c/strong\u003e The panel reviews the change, each model with a fresh context. They look for bugs, scope creep, and over-engineering. Extra complexity counts as a defect.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRefute.\u003c/strong\u003e More reviewers also mean more false alarms. So the same panel tries to prove each finding wrong.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eJudge.\u003c/strong\u003e One judge model checks each finding against the code. Is it real? Is it in scope? Is the proposed fix over-engineered? Then it rules: fix, reject, or defer. Deferred items go to human review.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFix.\u003c/strong\u003e The implementer fixes each accepted issue and runs the tests again.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe loop repeats until a round finds nothing serious.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27777-who-builds-who-reviews-4\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27777-who-builds-who-reviews-4\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eWho Builds, Who Reviews\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe loop has two jobs, and they need different models. The \u003cstrong\u003eimplementer\u003c/strong\u003e writes and fixes the code. It does most of the work, so it uses most of the tokens. It must be capable, but depending on the task, a premium model can be overkill. The \u003cstrong\u003epanel\u003c/strong\u003e reviews. Its models must be strong, and they must come from different companies, so their blind spots differ.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplementers\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"md-table\"\u003e\n\u003ctable\u003e\n\u003cthead\u003e\n\u003ctr\u003e\n\u003cth\u003eWork\u003c/th\u003e\n\u003cth\u003eModel\u003c/th\u003e\n\u003c/tr\u003e\n\u003c/thead\u003e\n\u003ctbody\u003e\n\u003ctr\u003e\n\u003ctd\u003eMost Issues\u003c/td\u003e\n\u003ctd\u003eMost of the work is everyday features, bug fixes, and refactors. Use Claude Opus, GPT Sol, or Grok. Open-weight models also work well here: Kimi, GLM, DeepSeek, Qwen, MiniMax, etc.\u003c/td\u003e\n\u003c/tr\u003e\n\u003ctr\u003e\n\u003ctd\u003eComplex or Difficult Issues\u003c/td\u003e\n\u003ctd\u003eArchitecture changes and hard-to-find bugs need the strongest implementer. Use Claude Opus or GPT Sol.\u003c/td\u003e\n\u003c/tr\u003e\n\u003ctr\u003e\n\u003ctd\u003eUI and Visual Work\u003c/td\u003e\n\u003ctd\u003eUse Claude Opus.\u003c/td\u003e\n\u003c/tr\u003e\n\u003ctr\u003e\n\u003ctd\u003eLong-Form Writing\u003c/td\u003e\n\u003ctd\u003eThis includes blog posts and page content. Use Claude Opus.\u003c/td\u003e\n\u003c/tr\u003e\n\u003ctr\u003e\n\u003ctd\u003eSmall, Non-Functional Fixes\u003c/td\u003e\n\u003ctd\u003eLabels, comments, and typos need little skill. Use a smaller model, such as GPT Luna or Claude Sonnet.\u003c/td\u003e\n\u003c/tr\u003e\n\u003c/tbody\u003e\n\u003c/table\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePanel\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA normal panel has three seats: one each from Anthropic, OpenAI, and SpaceXAI. One seat goes to a premium model. A hard problem gets all five.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"lightbox-wrapper\"\u003e\u003ca class=\"lightbox\" href=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/original/2X/2/234b13c54faa6ca5a74e736283dcd34c648325a0.png\" data-download-href=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/234b13c54faa6ca5a74e736283dcd34c648325a0\" title=\"Three panels. The Astra Panel has three seats: Claude Opus at high effort, GPT Astra at low effort, and Grok at high effort. The Fable Panel has three seats: Claude Fable at low effort, GPT Sol at high effort, and Grok at high effort. The Hard Problems panel has all five seats: Claude Opus, GPT Sol, and Grok at high effort, plus Claude Fable and GPT Astra at low effort. Claude Fable and GPT Astra are the premium models.\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/2/234b13c54faa6ca5a74e736283dcd34c648325a0_2_690x384.png\" alt=\"Three panels. The Astra Panel has three seats: Claude Opus at high effort, GPT Astra at low effort, and Grok at high effort. The Fable Panel has three seats: Claude Fable at low effort, GPT Sol at high effort, and Grok at high effort. The Hard Problems panel has all five seats: Claude Opus, GPT Sol, and Grok at high effort, plus Claude Fable and GPT Astra at low effort. Claude Fable and GPT Astra are the premium models.\" data-base62-sha1=\"52dyWODNTEKh2IVzr4eB7TqRcgU\" width=\"690\" height=\"384\" srcset=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/2/234b13c54faa6ca5a74e736283dcd34c648325a0_2_690x384.png, https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/2/234b13c54faa6ca5a74e736283dcd34c648325a0_2_1035x576.png 1.5x, https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/original/2X/2/234b13c54faa6ca5a74e736283dcd34c648325a0.png 2x\" data-dominant-color=\"EEF0F2\"\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"meta\"\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-far-image svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#far-image\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003cspan class=\"filename\"\u003eThree panels. The Astra Panel has three seats: Claude Opus at high effort, GPT Astra at low effort, and Grok at high effort. The Fable Panel has three seats: Claude Fable at low effort, GPT Sol at high effort, and Grok at high effort. The Hard Problems panel has all five seats: Claude Opus, GPT Sol, and Grok at high effort, plus Claude Fable and GPT Astra at low effort. Claude Fable and GPT Astra are the premium models.\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"informations\"\u003e1040×580 57.9 KB\u003c/span\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-discourse-expand svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#discourse-expand\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePremium models are stronger but cost much more, so they run at low effort. Even at low effort, they offer significant benefit because their baseline knowledge is much higher. The other models run at high effort. If the panel still disagrees after three rounds, another premium model joins the debate to break the tie. If the disagreement survives that, it goes to human review.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWhy not only premium models?\u003c/strong\u003e Premium models are the strongest, so one premium model, or a panel of only premium models, seems like enough. Testing shows otherwise. The lighter models still sometimes catch issues that the premium models miss, often enough to earn their seats.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWhy these three companies?\u003c/strong\u003e Open-weight models from Chinese labs add little to a panel. On reviews and hard problems, they rarely find anything that the OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceXAI models have not already found. This may be because they were distilled from models produced by these three frontier companies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27777-not-just-code-5\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27777-not-just-code-5\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eNot Just Code\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe same loop works for design. One model drafts a plan, a design, or a recommendation. The panel attacks it and works toward the best version. The draft reaches a human only once nothing serious is left. The next chart started this way. Three models each proposed a design, then debated the best approach until they agreed unanimously.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27777-what-reaches-human-review-6\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27777-what-reaches-human-review-6\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eWhat Reaches Human Review\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"lightbox-wrapper\"\u003e\u003ca class=\"lightbox\" href=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/original/2X/3/3b17f3dc2cedf59519b932c0296dd5091dce339d.png\" data-download-href=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/3b17f3dc2cedf59519b932c0296dd5091dce339d\" title=\"Both setups start with the same 6 bugs and 4 decisions. One model reviewing itself resolves 3 bugs and 1 decision, but its fixes add 4 new bugs, so 7 bugs and 3 decisions go to human review. Crossfire resolves 5 bugs and 2 decisions, and its fixes add 1 new bug, so 2 bugs and 2 decisions go to human review.\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/3/3b17f3dc2cedf59519b932c0296dd5091dce339d_2_690x493.png\" alt=\"Both setups start with the same 6 bugs and 4 decisions. One model reviewing itself resolves 3 bugs and 1 decision, but its fixes add 4 new bugs, so 7 bugs and 3 decisions go to human review. Crossfire resolves 5 bugs and 2 decisions, and its fixes add 1 new bug, so 2 bugs and 2 decisions go to human review.\" data-base62-sha1=\"8qLujp4MIAhIkrCDGmbELExAC9D\" width=\"690\" height=\"493\" srcset=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/3/3b17f3dc2cedf59519b932c0296dd5091dce339d_2_690x493.png, https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/3/3b17f3dc2cedf59519b932c0296dd5091dce339d_2_1035x739.png 1.5x, https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/original/2X/3/3b17f3dc2cedf59519b932c0296dd5091dce339d.png 2x\" data-dominant-color=\"F0F1F4\"\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"meta\"\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-far-image svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#far-image\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003cspan class=\"filename\"\u003eBoth setups start with the same 6 bugs and 4 decisions. One model reviewing itself resolves 3 bugs and 1 decision, but its fixes add 4 new bugs, so 7 bugs and 3 decisions go to human review. Crossfire resolves 5 bugs and 2 decisions, and its fixes add 1 new bug, so 2 bugs and 2 decisions go to human review.\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"informations\"\u003e1040×744 36.4 KB\u003c/span\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-discourse-expand svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#discourse-expand\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe last column is what goes to human review. Crossfire cannot resolve everything, but the combined panel leaves far less behind. Humans still set the project direction and make the difficult decisions. Most other issues are resolved before the work reaches them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27777-counting-the-real-cost-7\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27777-counting-the-real-cost-7\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eCounting the Real Cost\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCrossfire is more expensive than the usual method, where one model writes the code and a human reviews every change. Running multiple frontier models on every change means paying for multiple subscriptions, and that cost adds up fast. But the cost is a tradeoff. In return, you get fewer bugs and better code, and you move significantly faster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAn engineer’s time costs more than subscriptions. At Sōnami, the loop removes many days of unnecessary review each month for every engineer. Put a price on those days, and the subscriptions look small. In most cases, the extra cost is easily justified.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou are still the bottleneck. Crossfire makes sure your time is used only for the work that actually needs you.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe subscriptions cost money. The reviews you never have to do are worth more.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow do you review AI-written code today? We would like to hear what works for you.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003chr\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eThis post was originally published on the Sōnami blog: \u003ca href=\"https://blog.sonami.cc/posts/crossfire-loop-multi-model-review/\" class=\"inline-onebox\"\u003eCrossfire Loop: You Are the Bottleneck | Sōnami Blog\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":2,"updated_at":"2026-11-24T00:42:04.805Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":2,"reads":9,"readers_count":8,"score":56.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":11358,"topic_slug":"crossfire-loop-you-are-the-bottleneck","display_username":"Jordan Mack","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://blog.sonami.cc/posts/crossfire-loop-multi-model-review/","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"Crossfire Loop: You Are the Bottleneck | Sōnami 